# ---------------------------------
#   src/packages/kdos-appbox/catalogue — every application, as packages
# ---------------------------------
#
#   <kind>  <id>  <parent>  <apt packages …>
#
# `kind` is base | runtime | app | data. `parent` is the row this one is built
# ON TOP of: the device builds a stack of podman images, one per row, so a
# runtime's bytes are stored once however many applications sit on it.
#
# ROWS MAY STAND IN ANY ORDER, with one exception. The whole file is read
# before any chain is resolved, and a parent is looked up by id wherever it
# stands. A chain that names a parent not in the file, or runs past 16 rows
# (which is also where a cycle ends), cannot be closed, and the install stops
# for that application rather than looping.
#
# THE EXCEPTION IS `meta`: it is applied as it is read, so it must come AFTER
# the pack row it describes. One placed above that row is dropped without a
# message and the application presents as its bare id.
#
# A row's third field is a ROW id here, never an image tag. The builder
# resolves it to `kdos/<id>`, the image it built for that row.
#
# THIS FILE IS SHIPPED. It installs to /usr/share/kdos/appstore/catalogue and
# is what the store, kinstall and `kdos app` all read. There is no second copy
# and no generated index: a row added here is offered by every surface.
#
# `snapshot` pins the Debian archive the packages come from. `auto` takes the
# date from the base image's own sources file, so what is installed on top
# cannot disagree with the rootfs under it. A literal 20260824T000000Z pins
# explicitly; `off` reads the live archive and gives up reproducibility.
snapshot = auto

#
# `env <pack> NAME=VALUE` becomes an `env =` line in that pack's metadata, and
# kdos-appbox exports the whole stack's worth when it enters a pack box. A pack
# box is `podman --rootfs`, so there is no image whose labels could carry the
# fact: it has to travel with the packages it describes.
#
# QT_STYLE_OVERRIDE is only safe where a platform theme is present — Fusion
# with none falls back to Qt's built-in LIGHT palette, which is worse than
# leaving it alone. rt-qt has qgtk3, so it is safe there; rt-kde needs no
# override, because Breeze reads the same kdeglobals `kdos theme` writes.
env rt-qt   QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=gtk3
env rt-qt   QT_STYLE_OVERRIDE=Fusion
env rt-kde  QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=kde
# heaptrack-gui is the one Qt5 application on rt-kde, and trixie's
# plasma-integration is Qt6-only — under `kde` it has no platform theme and
# comes up in Qt's light palette. It carries qgtk3 and takes rt-qt's route.
env app.heaptrack QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=gtk3
env app.heaptrack QT_STYLE_OVERRIDE=Fusion
# A JACK client in a box reaches the host's PipeWire through pipewire-jack's
# libjack, which Debian installs beside the real one and selects with pw-jack
# — this is what pw-jack sets, for every program on the media runtime.
env rt-media LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pipewire-0.3/jack
# surf IS AN X11 CLIENT AND CANNOT BE TALKED OUT OF IT. It calls
# gdk_x11_window_get_xid() and gtk_plug_new() to hang its URI and find
# properties off a window id — that is how `tabbed` embeds it and how a script
# drives it — so GTK choosing the Wayland backend is an abort and not a
# fallback. Xwayland is the carve-out this rides on, and it is built with
# glamor and DRI3, so WebKit's EGL renderer is accelerated there; only the GLX
# clients lose out. This is an env line and NOT an `env GDK_BACKEND=x11`
# prefix in the Exec, which the launcher generator strips on sight.
env app.surf GDK_BACKEND=x11

# `deb <pack> <releases-api> <asset-pattern>` — for an application Debian does
# not carry. The newest asset matching the pattern is fetched and handed to
# apt, so its dependencies resolve normally.
deb app.vscodium https://api.github.com/repos/VSCodium/vscodium/releases/latest amd64.deb

